window.docContents[153].resolve({"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.CryptoGames.KEM.Kyber1024KeyGen/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___CryptoGames___KEM___Kyber1024KeyGen":{"contents":"Paper › LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames/KEM › Kyber1024KeyGen.leanTier 2 · definitions\n\nTier 2 · definitions used by the computational proof 2 headline theorems depend on it 88 of 134 lines trusted trust banner B2, trusted: definitions used by the statement source ↗Read the definitions shown. Only the declarations that the headline statements use are shown, with the namespace, section, open and variable commands needed to read them. The collapsed runs are theorems, which Lean checks, and definitions that no headline statement uses.\n\nRound-3 CRYSTALS-Kyber-1024 Key Generation and Noise-Explicit K-PKE Failure SpecificationTrusted: definitions used by the statement. Extends CryptoGames.MLKEM (LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames/KEM/MlKem.lean) with Round-3 CRYSTALS-Kyber-1024 key generation (kyber1024KeyPairWith, kyber1024KeyPair, and the UInt8 adapter kyber1024KemKeyGen, matching libcrux_ml_kem::mlkem1024::portable::kyber_generate_key_pair and the official Round-3 NIST PQCkemKAT_3168.rsp vectors) together with the single noise-explicit honest K-PKE decryption-failure predicate kyber1024KpkeFailsWith and real SHAKE-256 noise extractors (kyber1024RealNoiseKG, kyber1024RealNoiseEnc) read by SignalPqxdh.KyberNoiseGap (Computational/Specifications/Assumptions/Kyber1024Correct.lean). See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md and Computational/docs/DEVIATIONS.md (DEV-PQ21).\n\n\n\nRound-3 CRYSTALS-Kyber-1024 Key Generation (K = 4, η_1 = 2)\n\ndef kyber1024KeyPairWithL38–60 ↗#mlkem1024::kyber::generate_key_pair ((pk [1568B], sk [3168B])) parameterized by sampler. Models Round-3 CRYSTALS-Kyber-1024 (Kyber v3.02 Algorithms 4 and 7; libcrux variant::Kyber): expands (ρ, σ) = SHA3-512(d) without the FIPS 203 domain-separation byte k = 4, samples Â via sampleMatrixWith sampler 4 ρ true, samples s, e via sampleVecCbd2, and packs pk = ByteEncode_12(t̂) ‖ ρ (1568 bytes) and sk = ByteEncode_12(ŝ) ‖ pk ‖ SHA3-256(pk) ‖ z (3168 bytes).\n\ndef kyber1024KeyPairL62–69 ↗#Closed Round-3 CRYSTALS-Kyber-1024 key generation (List (BitVec 8) view), normalizing the input seed to 64 bytes via toByteVec 64 and the output (pk, sk) to (1568B, 3168B) via toByteVec, using the same unbounded sampleNtt matrix sampler as kyber1024Encaps and kyber1024Decaps.\n\ndef kyber1024KemKeyGenL71–77 ↗#UInt8 byte-list adapter for Round-3 CRYSTALS-Kyber-1024 key generation (List UInt8 → List UInt8 × List UInt8), matching SignalPqxdh.Primitives.kemKeyGen and CryptoVerification.E2E.UnreachedPqxdhPrimitives.kemKeyGen with exact wire lengths (1568, 3168).\n\nNoise-Explicit Honest K-PKE Failure Event (kyber1024KpkeFailsWith)\n\ndef kyber1024RealNoiseKGL81–85 ↗#Real SHAKE-256 CBD_2 input blocks (8 × 128 bytes: nonces 0..3 for s, 4..7 for e) derived in kyber1024KeyPairWith from σ = SHA3-512(seed64[0..32])[32..64].\n\ndef kyber1024RealNoiseEncL87–94 ↗#Real SHAKE-256 CBD_2 input blocks (9 × 128 bytes: nonces 0..3 for r, 4..7 for e_1, 8 for e_2) derived in kyber1024EncapsWith from pk1568 and rand32 via m = SHA3-256(rand32[0..32]) and coins = SHA3-512(m ‖ SHA3-256(pk1568[0..1568]))[32..64].\n\ndef kyber1024KpkeFailsWithL96–132 ↗#Single noise-explicit Round-3 CRYSTALS-Kyber-1024 K-PKE honest decryption-failure predicate, used identically for both worlds of SignalPqxdh.KyberNoiseGap: runs key generation from seed64 with explicit CBD_2 byte blocks nKG (0..3 for s, 4..7 for e), encrypts m = SHA3-256(rand32[0..32]) under the resulting pk with explicit CBD_2 byte blocks nEnc (0..3 for r, 4..7 for e_1, 8 for e_2), and tests whether indCpaDecrypt 4 11 5 fails to recover m.\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames/KEM/Kyber1024KeyGen.lean","id":"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.CryptoGames.KEM.Kyber1024KeyGen/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___CryptoGames___KEM___Kyber1024KeyGen"},"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.CryptoGames.Ratchet.LibsignalSessionSM/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___CryptoGames___Ratchet___LibsignalSessionSM":{"contents":"Paper › LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames/Ratchet › LibsignalSessionSM.leanTier 2 · definitions\n\nTier 2 · definitions used by the computational proof and symbolic proof 7 headline theorems depend on it 112 of 162 lines trusted source ↗trust banner in the header: B2, trusted: definitions used by the statement; the closure computes B3 for the symbolic proofRead the definitions shown. Only the declarations that the headline statements use are shown, with the namespace, section, open and variable commands needed to read them. The collapsed runs are theorems, which Lean checks, and definitions that no headline statement uses.\n\nDeclarations on this page (9)structure GhostStatestructure InitKeysstructure Paramsdef Params.sendAddrsdef Params.expectedAddrsdef initBdef initAdef senddef receive\n\nThe libsignal session as an SM scheme with receive coins (the adapter)Trusted: definitions used by the statement. The theorems of Computational/Theorems/ use every definition in this file: read them. The theorems here are checked by Lean. See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md.Adapts the pure session model of LibsignalSession.lean to the receive-coin secure-messaging components (initA, initB, send, receive; DEV-LS1, DEV-LS3, DEV-LS4, DEV-LS17) over GhostState (attaching ghost epoch numbers to ratchet-key-indexed chains, DEV-LS8, DEV-LS18) and abstract PQXDH InitKeys (DEV-LS22, DEV-LS28), preserving unacknowledged address checks (DEV-LS15) and clone-based atomic decryption failure (DEV-LS16).\n\n\n\nstructure GhostStateL34–51 ↗#The adapter state: a libsignal session plus ghost epochs.DEV-LS8 (libsignal): epochs are named by the ratchet key, not numbered. ACD19 Fig. 8 carries the epoch number t in the header (§5.2 \"Epoch indexing\"). libsignal identifies an epoch by the sender's ratchet public key, so the model adds ghost epochs (epoch, chainEpochs). The MAC binds the epoch only through its parity (identity-key order), so the per-message AEAD bounds are stated for adversaries that stay in one epoch (AllQueriesInOneADClass), and ratchet-key collisions cost a term (DEV-LS18). Effect: constants.\n\nstructure InitKeysL53–75 ↗#The abstract initial keys (PQXDH is out of scope; DEV-LS28): the PQXDH root key, chain key and SPQR key pqr_key (both parties' SPQR states are spqr.init dir pqrKey, ratchet.rs:83–96, 146–159), the coins of Bob's signed pre-key (B's initial ratchet key pair, A's initial \"their ratchet key\"), and the coins of Alice's eager first ratchet key pair.DEV-LS22 (libsignal): Bob's signed pre-key public key is secret in the model. ACD19 models CKA as a secret-key primitive: the initial key k_CKA = (h, x₀) with h = g^{x₀} is shared and secret (§4.1.2, §5.2 \"Initial key agreement\"). In libsignal h is Bob's signed pre-key, published in his pre-key bundle and reused across sessions. Here its coins are fresh, independent and uniform per session, and only corr-B reveals it. Effect: none relative to ACD19, but weaker than deployed libsignal: no claim against adversaries that know Bob's signed pre-key in advance. DEV-LS3, DEV-LS4 and the epoch-0 term exist because of it.\n\nstructure ParamsL77–88 ↗#Fixed session parameters: primitives, protobuf codec, SPQR, identity keys, and serialized address blobs (none when unserializable).\n\ndef Params.sendAddrsL90–93 ↗#The address blob a party sends (if its session is unacknowledged).\n\ndef Params.expectedAddrsL95–98 ↗#The address blob a party expects.\n\n\n\ndef initBL104–107 ↗#Init-B: initialize_recipient_session with Bob's signed pre-key pair; epoch 0, no chains.\n\ndef initAL109–119 ↗#Init-A: initialize_initiator_session (eager first sending chain) and set_unacknowledged_pre_key_message; epoch 0, one receiver chain (Bob's signed pre-key, ghost epoch 0).\n\ndef sendL121–136 ↗#Send(st, m; r): message_encrypt with SPQR coins r and the party's address blob; the ghost epoch moves to the next sending epoch of the party if it is in a receiving epoch (Fig. 8's ep-mgmt). On failure: the unchanged state and the empty wire.DEV-LS17 (formalization): adapter conventions. ACD19 Def. 6's Send always produces a ciphertext. Here send is total: if message_encrypt fails it returns the old state and the empty wire, while the game records a send (honest runs never fail under Corr.SpqrOffCorrect). initA falls back to a session without a sender chain if the initial X25519 agreement is all zero, and received plaintexts pass through padOrTruncatePlain ℓ (libsignalSessionScheme). Effect: none.\n\ndef receiveL138–157 ↗#Rcv(st, c; rc): SignalMessage::try_from and try_decrypt_from_record with eager coins rc and the party's expected address blob. On success: the new state (ghost epochs pushed / evicted with the receiver chains), the ghost epoch of the chain used (epoch + 1 for a new chain, which also becomes the party's epoch), the index counter + 1, and the plaintext. Any failure is none (the record is unchanged).\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames/Ratchet/LibsignalSessionSM.lean","id":"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.CryptoGames.Ratchet.LibsignalSessionSM/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___CryptoGames___Ratchet___LibsignalSessionSM"}});